      {
        "ballot_id": "B004",
        "laureates": [
          "Charles L. Kane",
          "Eugene J. Mele",
          "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
        ],
        "citation": "for the quantum spin Hall effect and topological insulators"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B022",
    "B034"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B004 remains my preferred achievement. Kane and Mele provided the seminal quantum-spin-Hall theory, while Molenkamp led a defining experimental realization. It established a new, experimentally robust classification of electronic phases, with transport signatures and topological interpretation tested extensively over two decades. The configuration has an unusually coherent connection between a precise theoretical prediction and a decisive materials experiment.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation is attribution: the broader topological-insulator history includes closely related theoretical and experimental advances, so the citation must be delimited to the quantum spin Hall effect and the named experimental realization rather than claiming all of topological matter. Optical lattice clocks offer a more compact attribution and an equally mature case.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll places B022 first, emphasizing Katori's magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept, Ye's foundational experimental advances, and the platform's long-established reproducibility and metrological impact.",
        "response": "I agree that B022 is an exceptionally strong alternative with clear two-person attribution and mature performance. My qualification is that its most far-reaching fundamental discoveries remain prospective, whereas B004 already represents a demonstrated new quantum phase and a durable conceptual reorganization of electronic materials."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson ranks B003 first because squeezed light made quantum fluctuations a controllable precision-measurement resource, combining Caves's quantum-noise framework with major experimental realization.",
        "response": "The physical achievement and its deployment are compelling, but Johansson rightly notes that experimental credit extends beyond the proposed names. That unresolved division among generation, interferometric application, and other pioneers makes B003 less cleanly attributable than B004 at this stage."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson ranks B034 first, viewing aberration correction as a mature transformation of electron microscopy into a routine atomic-scale materials tool, centered on Rose and Haider.",
        "response": "I share the high assessment of B034 and its importance for materials physics. Its historical credit, however, must distinguish optical concepts, corrector realization, and instrumental development; B004 currently has the tighter discovery-to-attribution narrative."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether Molenkamp is the most appropriate sole experimental representative once the full history of quantum-spin-Hall observations and related topological-insulator work is reviewed.",
      "How narrowly a final citation must be framed to avoid overlap with other topological-matter achievements."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
